greetings my name is don  alexander and i'm president and managing  director of gene  cota professional and executive search  our firm connects outstanding firms in  the life sciences  industry with executives who help bring  game changing products to the market  before we get started today i wanted to  take the opportunity to thank  lab roots drug discovery and development  for hosting this virtual  event code 19 has changed the way  companies  operate the way employees perform their  work in the way  both are adapting to this environment  in this session we'll cover both macro  industry trends  and specific changes enabling attendees  to gain a holistic perspective  in this presentation you're going to  learn about  the current life sciences job market  best practices in recruitment  interviewing and hiring  employee and candidate sentiment  and what i call the new normal landscape  so let's begin with an overview of the  current life sciences job market  our firm produces a quarterly report  called life sciences pulse  a link to this complementary report can  be found at the bottom of this slide  the life sciences pulse report depicts  all online job  postings or advertisements for the life  sciences sector  in the united states over the past three  years  including trending patterns and sector  sub-components please note that we will  begin  charging a fee for this report in the  second quarter of this year but you can  presently download a copy  and be grandfathered in for future  releases at no charge  online ads are a leading indicator  in employment in the hiring cycle  as such sector posting data provides  meaningful market activity and direction  that can assist business leaders in the  life sciences industry  to make informed decisions regarding  human capital  so let's talk a little bit about our  methods  of data collection and analysis  in this report so data contained in this  report was sourced from a company called  mz or emsi  mz compiles an extensive sweep of all  online posted positions or ads in the us  on a monthly basis with the ability to  review  up to four years of prior data  mz data is derived from quarterly  updates  of 18 billion data points curated from  dozens of government data sources as  well as hundreds of millions of online  job  postings with filters by company  job title skills keywords  and more offering a wealth of insight  into the us life sciences labor market  i think it's also important to speak  about the u.s life sciences sector  overview  and definition mz categorizes  industries by leveraging the north  american  industry classification system code  or what's commonly referred to as naix  while nax codes are considered the most  reliable source of data  and industry classification in the  united states  there are occasions where industries may  be over-represented  or under-represented based on  industry definitions these issues most  often occur  where there's an attempt to capture high  technology  or emerging growth segments of markets  that are not  as clearly defined as say mature  industries  to accurately define the u.s life  sciences sector  based on product development gene coda  consulted the  teconomy bio investment  innovation and job creation in a growing  u.s  biosciences industry 2018 report  for guidance and then reviewed the focus  set of 24 nex  codes found in that report we then  adjusted to  15 ax codes which are listed in our  report  and deal principally with the product  development side of the industry  today we're going to be reviewing the  fourth quarter of 2020's  numbers the fourth quarter report hasn't  been released yet  but it will be available at the  beginning of february  so with that let's take a look  the slide you're now looking at is a  picture of all posted ads  in our definition of the life sciences  industry  on a quarterly year-over-year basis  with the current quarter labeled in blue  and the same quarter in the prior year  in red when reviewing figure one  we note that employers posted 537  9882 ads in the life sciences sector  in q4 of 2020 and that's up from  492 606 ads  in q3 of 2020.  we see that q4 of 2020 represented  an approximate 14 percent  year-over-year growth or increase in ads  posted in the sector  which was really quite frankly  remarkable growth  as you look at the quarterly comparisons  please also note that the second quarter  of 2020 represented the first  full quarter of results in the pandemic  time frame so when koba began  each successive quarter since q2  of 2020 has moved you'll notice in a  stepwise  upwardly fashion denoting a quarter  where hiring took a pause in q2  and then resumed its upward trajec  trajectory rather  so in this particular slide and picture  we'll take a look at some of the  sub-sector components  that make up the life sciences industry  from le from left to right rather the  red bar graph  rtm stands for research  testing and medical laboratories  the blue bar graph pmm  stands for pharmaceutical medicine  manufacturing  and finally the green bar stands for  medical devices  and equipment when you add these three  sub sectors together they make up the  entire life sciences industry  as we've defined it in the report  we also find that for our audience it's  helpful to think about some of the  industry companies or names logos if you  will  that make up each sub sector so  rtm for instance example companies might  include  laboratory corporation of america quest  iqvia and covants  pmm sector example companies  would include pfizer johnson and johnson  abbvie and merck and finally  the mde sector companies might include  thermo fisher scientific medtronic  striker and donahue  so what insights and conclusions can we  draw about the state  of the life sciences job market  number one the life sciences job market  has recovered significantly  since the second quarter of 2020 when  the  covet 19 pandemic really truly began  q4 of 2020 was the second highest  quarterly tally  in the last three years so a significant  comeback  in q4 of this year  the medical device segment or mde  skyrocketed forward a stunning 27  percent  from the third quarter of 2020  the pharmaceutical and medicine  manufacturing or pmm  segments and the rtm or research testing  and medical  lab segments each had healthy gains  of about five percent each over this  third quarter as well  so what do we expect near term at a  grassroots level  we're now seeing several companies  adding key staff  several companies we have spoken with  are resuming hiring  that may have been delayed during the  pandemic  which will result in more near-term  competition for talent  and ultimately higher industry wages  although coded spread continues to  remain a significant variable  with the election season now behind us  our expectation in q1 is for modest  growth  we expect modest upside in the pmm  segment more significant relative growth  in the rtm segment and maybe a little  bit of cooling off  a quarter in the mde segment from the q4  2020 time frame after all it's hard to  beat a 20 plus percent uh quarterly gain  [Music]  so next when we consider advice for  candidates who may be actively  interviewing  the boy scouts of america motto be  prepared  comes to mind we suggest  number one practice and rehearsal of  both  video interviews as well as potential  presentations  ask a friend to set up a mock interview  and give you feedback  for example secondly  we want to make sure and check all of  our technology  check mics and video cam operations  including lighting as well as internet  connectivity  and application download of the given  technology  such as zoom and do this ahead of an  interview to minimize  the possibility of disruption  thirdly we want to always dress  appropriately  for a zoom interview after all it is an  in-person interview  we also suggest the use of a virtual  background or professional home  office setting so companies we find  tend to be more forgiving in video  interviewing  given our present circumstances  but we do think that one still needs to  be cognizant of what a background  looks like or what their background  looks like as well as making  every effort to minimize the chances for  disruption such as the dogs barking in  the background  if you believe a potential disruption  might occur let the interview know  as things begin and explain how you've  been able to make the shift  from or to rather working from home  in spite of potential distractions  now also don't forget to turn off mobile  phones or computer sounds  that may be disruptive  also and we'll see this more i think as  the 2021 year progresses  i would note on this that we haven't  seen  or had a candidate travel request really  from a client  since this past march but i have asked  candidates if they are willing to meet  in person  on client request assuming cdc  guidelines  are followed as well  but do be cognizant that as the year  progresses  as the vaccine is manufactured and  distributed more broadly  that we may begin to resume in-person  interviewing i think that's important to  to think about  so um when we consider how the need to  uh work remotely has  changed hiring practices or selection  criteria for candidates  we note that more companies may seek  insight on how candidates have  effectively managed  their work from home situations  especially if they  haven't been accustomed to this in the  past  companies might inquire about the  environment in which one  has worked is this been primarily office  based  you know again precovered and asked  about  how an individual's managed to adjust  to a holy or primarily work from home  environment for example a client might  ask  if i understand correctly you've been  working from a home office exclusively  over the past six months to which you  might respond  and then you know what has been your  biggest challenge  in making that transition and what have  you done  to overcome this challenge  those are the kinds of questions that we  may see  in some of the interviews that we'll be  participating in  now with respect to employee and  candidate sentiment  we've seen a few key themes develop  during the pandemic  first and foremost people are not likely  to  relocate during the pandemic  so preparation for remote work  initially and subsequent relocation  is important to contemplate for both  employers  and employees alike  second people that are employed today  are less likely to be probing the  marketplace right now  and tend to be more settled into their  current work environment  therefore thinking about proactive  outreach becomes  very important when considering passive  job seekers  third in terms of understanding  work-life  balance we would encourage employers to  understand that  candidates and employees have now  established a  rhythm if you will that works for them  and works for their family  so if they make a change this could be  disruptive  it's important to understand what this  change  might look like from a new employee's  point of view  finally we are seeing that companies who  provide  flexibility on the site  work from home different requirements  different scenarios  but we're finding that the more flexible  that the client employer is the better  able  they are to attract talent  now when we think about the new normal  and kind of what this might look like in  terms of recruiting  hiring and onboarding we're really  seeing several  key themes emerge  the new normal for recruiting and hiring  will include a candidate's ability to  both interview  and work remotely  and you know that's going to be for  a company's ability to help manage this  new normal  for recruiting we're seeing the an  increased  use i suppose of virtual job fairs  as well as the use of various types of  videos  that would be shared with candidates  for onboarding we're also seeing key  themes such as  potential temperature checks and cova  testing  for on-site work again this is  organizationally dependent  but being aware that one's temperature  may be checked  and that one may be subject to coveted  testing  requirements is important we're also  seeing  the use of tools like docusign used to  onboard employees in a more efficient  manner  and we're also seeing that employers are  leveraging  digital tools handbooks and information  for training purposes since a new  employee  can't readily ask their neighbor you  know quite as easily these days  you know all the way in in really what  i guess we're starting to see occur and  this is what i would really consider to  be  a sea change in the work environment  is that many companies will accelerate  their ability to hire candidates no  matter where they're located  and we've already seen over the past few  years a trend in this area but covet and  the use of these video technologies has  really  accelerated this trend although there  may be  preference or requirements in some cases  for candidates  to be located in specific areas for  specific reasons such as being in the  same time zone  or having to physically come in to a lab  to perform various experiments  companies and employers alike will be  able to hire a much broader pool  of candidates which is really good for  both parties  a recent zillow article stated that  a switch to more telework could give  1.92 million u.s renters or  four and a half percent of renter  households  the option to leave the metropolitan  areas  where they currently live and buy a you  know a starter home in a cheaper  locale and that close to  half of u.s workers  are in occupations in which teleworkings  at least theoretically feasible the less  than a quarter of these workers  actually telework so a lot of room  to continue this trend so a ramification  of this new normal  will be migrations from higher cost of  living  metropolitan areas and we're already  really seeing this now  microsoft for example recently announced  that  it is allowing more of its employees to  work from home permanently  and managers can approve permanent  remote work  as companies in the tech industry follow  suit with microsoft and others  i wouldn't think that it would be long  before the remote work  framework encompasses a wider swath  of the life sciences sector  so finally i want to thank you for your  time  in attending this session and hope that  you enjoy the rest of the lab roots  drug discovery and development virtual  event

